You can't — Vite maintainers declined the callback approach. As bluwy explained, "We don't want to accept a function for now as it's difficult to merge if other plugins were to add more conditions" [5]. Instead, import the default list and spread it yourself: use `defaultClientConditions` from Vite for client builds, or `defaultServerConditions` for server builds [5].

```js
import { defaultClientConditions } from 'vite'

export default defineConfig({
  resolve: {
    conditions: ['workspace', ...defaultClientConditions],
  },
})
```

`defaultClientConditions` is documented at https://vite.dev/config/shared-options#resolve-conditions [5], and a user confirmed this approach "works perfectly" for their use case [4].

The failure is caused by an upstream Rolldown issue (rolldown/rolldown#9964), specifically the `lazyBarrel` feature [2]. You can work around it by disabling that feature in your config [2][5]:

```js
export default {
  build: {
    rolldownOptions: {
      experimental: { lazyBarrel: false }
    }
  }
}
```

Alternatively, upgrading to Rolldown v1.1.4 should work, since the lazyBarrel feature was temporarily disabled upstream [1].

The failure is a Rolldown CJS namespace interop issue, not something specific to `backbone-relational`: a CJS namespace import snapshots the current `module.exports` properties before a later CJS side-effect import mutates the same export object [3]. That's why `import * as Backbone from 'backbone'` followed by `import 'backbone-relational'` works in Vite dev but fails in Vite 8 production builds, while default and named imports still work because they keep/read the original CJS export object [3]. The durable fix is expected upstream in Rolldown [3].

As a workaround, switch to a default import [1]:

```js
import Backbone from "backbone";
import "backbone-relational";

Backbone.RelationalModel.extend({});
```

This is a reported Vite 8 bug: in `build()` library mode, `resolve.alias` is ignored unless you move or copy it under `build.rolldownOptions` [2]. The reporter notes the error is not reproduced at Vite 7 [1]. The workaround shown is to duplicate the alias into `build.rolldownOptions.resolve.alias` [2]:

```js
await build({
  configFile: false,
  resolve: { alias: { "@": path.resolve("./src") } },
  build: {
    rolldownOptions: {
      resolve: { alias: { "@": path.resolve("./src") } },
    },
    // ...
  }
})
```

The excerpts don't state the underlying cause, so I can't explain why it happens beyond it being a Vite 8 regression.

There's no built-in option yet — the `maxWarnings` config option is only a suggested solution in the open issue [2], and a maintainer prefers keeping it plugin-based for now [4]. The workaround is a plugin that counts warnings via `onLog` and throws in `buildEnd` [3]:

```ts
function failOnWarningsPlugin(): Rolldown.Plugin {
  let warningCount = 0;
  return {
    name: 'fail-on-warnings',
    onLog(level) {
      if (level === 'warn') warningCount++;
    },
    buildEnd() {
      if (!warningCount) return;
      throw new Error(`${warningCount} warnings present`);
    },
  };
}
```

This reports all warnings before failing, unlike throwing in rolldown's `onwarn` callback, which only shows the first [2]. Note that the thrown error will also log an error stack trace [5].

Vite treats `.jsx` and `.js` similarly, unlike webpack, and because your package.json has `"type": "module"`, the CommonJS module's default export isn't unwrapped automatically — so the import resolves to an object rather than the component, causing "Element type is invalid... but got: object" [2][4]. The fix is to access `.default` explicitly [4]:

```js
import FroalaEditorComponent from 'react-froala-wysiwyg';
const Editor = FroalaEditorComponent.default;
```

See the rolldown docs on "ambiguous default import from CJS modules" for details [4].

The wrong ordering comes from Rolldown's chunking: when you use `manualChunks` (which uses `codeSplitting` internally), side effects can be triggered before the corresponding modules are actually used, so a polyfill like `reflect-metadata` can be deferred until after a dependent module's top-level guard check [3][4]. A maintainer notes this is "kind of the same with Vite 7 (Rollup)," since Rollup's docs warn that manual chunks can change application behaviour if side effects fire before the modules are used [3]. It also relates to Rolldown's automatic code splitting, where respecting execution order doesn't take precedence [5]. The suggested workaround in Vite 8 (Rolldown) is `output.strictExecutionOrder` [3][5]:

```js
// vite.config.js
export default {
  build: {
    rollupOptions: {
      output: { strictExecutionOrder: true }
    }
  }
}
```

The Rolldown team says they'll keep working on reducing that option's downsides [5].

It's a TypeScript limitation, not a Vite bug: the async `defineConfig` callback's return type isn't inferred narrowly enough, so `minify: 'terser'` (or `'esbuild'`) fails type checking with error 2769 [3]. The fix is to add `as const` to the value [2]:

```ts
export default defineConfig(async () => ({
  plugins: [react()],
  build: {
    minify: 'terser' as const,
  },
}))
```

The issue is that Vite/Rolldown follows symlinks and resolves module IDs to their real paths outside the Bazel sandbox, so `normalizedId` ends up outside `config.root` [3][5]. A workaround is a `pre` plugin that remaps resolved IDs back into the sandbox directory [4]:

```js
export function bazelSandbox() {
  if (process.env.JS_BINARY__EXECROOT == null) return [];
  return [{
    name: "bazel-sandbox",
    enforce: "pre",
    async resolveId(source, importer, options) {
      const resolved = await this.resolve(source, importer, { ...options, skipSelf: true });
      if (resolved && !resolved.external) {
        resolved.id = bazelSandboxRemap(resolved.id);
      }
      return resolved;
    },
  }];
}
```

Per the thread, this should be fixed by the released 8.2.0 [2].

Yes. In rolldown-vite 7.1.8, comments are removed even when `minify: false` is set, so `/* @vite-ignore */` and `/* webpackIgnore: true */` annotations are stripped from the build output; in 7.1.7 they were preserved [3]. This is filed as a regression [1][3].

As a workaround, put the comment before the string rather than inside the `import()` call [5]:

```js
export async function init() {
  const bar = /* @vite-ignore */ /* webpackIgnore: true */ 'some-url'.slice(0);
  return await import(
    bar
  );
}
```

Vite doesn't hot reload on PHP edits because it doesn't know about `*.php` files and shouldn't treat them specially [5]. The behavior is specific to Tailwind, and a related undesired behavior is fixed by PR #20391, which is the likely cause of the change [1][4]. The recommended fix is to trigger a full reload via a custom plugin [1]:

```js
plugins: [
	{
		name: 'reload-non-vite-code',
		hotUpdate(ctx) {
			if (ctx.file.endsWith(".php")) {
				ctx.server.environments.client.hot.send({
					type: 'full-reload',
				})
			}
		},
	}
]
```

Alternatively, `vite-plugin-full-reload` should work [5].

The HMR/WebSocket server gets invoked twice because `createServer` is called twice—once for the client config and once for the server config—and each call starts its own WS server, so the listen function runs twice [2]. The reporter notes there's no need for a WebSocket server when the `ViteDevServer` is only used for `ssrLoadModule` [2]. A maintainer suggests that with the Env API you don't have to create multiple Vite servers, and that you can set `server.ws: false` to disable the WS server [1].

```js
// in the server-side config
export default {
  server: { ws: false }
}
```

The type error happens because Vite types the `importers` option from the `sass` package rather than `sass-embedded`, even though Vite prefers `sass-embedded` [1][3]. A maintainer suggests the fix is to swap the order so `sass-embedded` types are checked first [1][4]. Until that's fixed, the reported workaround is to cast the `sass-embedded` importer to the `sass` type [2][3]:

```ts
import {defineConfig} from 'vite';
import {NodePackageImporter} from 'sass-embedded';
import {NodePackageImporter as DartSassPackageImporter} from 'sass';

export default defineConfig({
  css: {
    preprocessorOptions: {
      scss: {
        api: 'modern-compiler',
        importers: [
          new NodePackageImporter() as unknown as DartSassPackageImporter,
        ],
      },
    },
  },
});
```

Note that using `NodePackageImporter` from `sass` directly instead causes a runtime error: `importer.findFileUrl is not a function` [3].

The error occurs because Vite does not support processing CJS modules, so a CJS module imported for SSR (e.g. JSDOM via a plugin) fails in dev with `ReferenceError: require is not defined` [4]. Externalizing the module should fix it, but resolving by Vite plus externalizing (returning `{ id: resolveId, external: true }` in the `resolveId` hook) does not work — likely related to #19461 [4]. The workaround is to make the virtual module a facade module [4]:

```js
const p = {
  name: 'server-only-module',
  async resolveId(id, importer, {ssr}) {
    const prefix = 'server-only:';
    if (id.startsWith(prefix)) {
      if (ssr) return id
      return '\0empty-module';
    }
  },
  load(id) {
    if (id === '\0empty-module') return 'export {}';
    const prefix = 'server-only:';
    if (id.startsWith(prefix)) {
      return `export * from ${JSON.stringify(id.slice(prefix.length))}`
    }
  },
}
```

The excerpts do not explain why `build && preview` works while dev fails beyond the CJS-processing limitation [3][4].

Vite's watcher does detect the change in the imported `.scss` file, but the CSS isn't correctly injected [1]. A Vite maintainer investigated and found that postcss was returning the same value even when the input changed, suggesting tailwind was caching results incorrectly, so it was closed as not a Vite bug [3]. Users confirmed the issue persisted after updating to tailwind v4.0.13 and tailwind/postcss v4.0.13, and the base issue was reopened in the Tailwind repo [4][5].

A partial workaround: rename the main style file to `.css` and use regular `@import` to include the `.scss` files, which triggers updates when those files change [2]:

```css
@import 'tailwindcss';

@import 'components/_simple-test.scss';
```

However, nested `.scss` imports using `@use` inside `.scss` files still won't trigger a CSS update unless the main scss file is updated [2].

There's no built-in option to disable CSS preloading yet; it's a feature request [1][5]. A maintainer noted Vite could add an option to disable automatic CSS loading, but wasn't sure it could be scoped to a specific import [2]. As a workaround, you can remove the import-analysis build plugin via a custom plugin [3]. Note that in Vite 8 the plugin name changed, so remove `native:import-analysis-build` instead of `vite:build-import-analysis` [3]:

```js
{
  name: 'preload-remover',
  configResolved(config) {
    const pI = config.plugins.findIndex(
      (p) => p.name === 'native:import-analysis-build',
    );
    (config.plugins as Plugin[]).splice(pI, 1);
  },
}
```

The hang is an upstream Rollup bug that surfaced in Vite v6 `--watch` mode; it was fixed in rollup v4.44.0 [2]. Until you're on that Rollup version, the workaround is to limit parallel file operations in your Vite config [1][3]:

```js
export default {
  build: {
    rollupOptions: {
      maxParallelFileOps: 100
    }
  }
}
```

Users report the hang occurring at CSS transformation, and that setting `maxParallelFileOps: 100` resolves it [1][3].

Because Sass's modern API strips the alias before Vite sees the path: it passes `@/../node_modules/bootstrap/scss/_functions` to Vite as `node_modules/bootstrap/scss/_functions`, so the relative resolution from the alias no longer works [4]. This is a limitation of Sass's modern API (the default from Vite 6) with no easy fix, so the issue was closed as won't-fix [2][3][4]. As a workaround, set the legacy API:

```js
// vite.config.js
export default {
  css: {
    preprocessorOptions: {
      sass: { api: 'legacy' }
    }
  }
}
```

(For SCSS, use `scss.api: 'legacy'` instead [1].)

The thread's workaround is a small Vite plugin that rewrites Prisma's `eval("__dirname")` to `import.meta.dirname` for the generated client [5]. An earlier version of the setup also rebased `require(".prisma/client/default")` to `../../.prisma/client/index` and replaced `__dirname` in `@prisma/client`/`.prisma/client` code, but that approach produced `SyntaxError: Cannot use 'import.meta' outside a module` on Vercel [2]. The contributor says the `import.meta.dirname` plugin is "the only plugin needed now" [5]:

```js
{
  name: "prisma:build",
  transform(code, id) {
    if (id.includes("client-generated")) {
      return code.replace('eval("__dirname")', "import.meta.dirname");
    }
  },
},
```

A possible remaining runtime problem is a missing Prisma binary (e.g. `node_modules/@prisma/client-generated/libquery_engine-darwin-arm64.dylib.node`); you can copy it in a `buildEnd()` hook or extend `.vercel/prepare.js`, placing it directly in the `index.func` directory outside `index.func/assets` [5]. The reporter confirmed this let them deploy to Vercel with a working Prisma Client in a serverless environment [4].

To disable HMR, use the `server.hmr: false` option [4]. For WebSocket specifically, a maintainer says there's no supported option and suggests using `vite preview` instead of `vite dev` if you're trying to emulate production [4]. As a workaround, one user patched `setupWebSocket` to return early and ignore the WebSocket [1][2]:

```js
function setupWebSocket(protocol, hostAndPath, onCloseWithoutOpen) {
    return null; // return and ignore websocket
    const socket = new WebSocket(`${protocol}://${hostAndPath}`,'vite-hmr');
    // ...
}
```
